<BlogArticle slug="jarvis-verse">
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Until now your Jarvis agents lived in a list. From today they live in an
    office. Personal Jarvis 2.4 ships Jarvis Verse: a walkable 3D office where
    every agent has a desk, and every coding agent works one floor up with its
    terminal live on its monitor.
  </Fragment>

  <BlogVideo
    slot="media"
    src="/blog/jarvis-verse/jarvis-verse-launch.mp4"
    poster="/blog/jarvis-verse/poster.webp"
    width={1920}
    height={1080}
    label="Launch film: a one-minute walk through both floors of Jarvis Verse."
    caption="The launch film: one minute through both floors of Jarvis Verse."
  />

  <h2>Why an office</h2>
  <p>
    A list tells you which agents exist. It does not tell you, at a glance, who
    is working, who is waiting for you and who has nothing to do. Jarvis Verse
    answers that in one picture. Every agent has a desk, desks are grouped into
    departments, and each figure shows its state where it is:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Working</strong> agents sit at their screen, and the monitor shows
      the latest lines of their chat as they come in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Waiting for you</strong> agents stand at their desk and wave.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Idle</strong> agents take a break: coffee, the couch, the window,
      the bookshelf, the water cooler or the arcade.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Paused</strong> agents nap on a couch.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The office shows your team as it is. It never starts, stops or invents
    work. The walking, the coffee breaks and the naps are animation, worked out
    by the app on your own machine, and they cost no tokens.
  </p>

  <h2>The agents floor</h2>
  <p>
    Each department is a model provider, so agents that run on the same
    provider sit together, local models through Ollama included. Around the
    desks are the rooms you use to run the team:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Spawn point.</strong> Hire a new agent here: give it a job, a
      brain and a look. It appears on the pad in the middle of the floor and
      walks to a free desk.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lead office.</strong> Jarvis, your lead agent, has the corner
      suite. Walk up to the desk and you can take the chair yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Team room.</strong> Pick two or more agents and they become a team
      with a shared chat. They meet at the table right away.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wardrobe.</strong> Dress your own character or any agent: outfit,
      colours, hair, skin tone and glasses.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reception and the agent board.</strong> Help, the full list of
      agents, and agent management.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Break room.</strong> Couches, a coffee bar, a water cooler and an
      arcade cabinet running a game called Asteroid Run.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is also an office dog. It moves between its baskets in the lead
    office, the team room and the break room. Pet it and it follows you around
    for a while. The treat jar next to the coffee bar is there for a reason.
  </p>

  <h2>The coding floor</h2>
  <p>
    Take the elevator up. Every coding agent running in the Agentic IDE, such
    as Claude Code, Codex or Gemini CLI, is a character here, and every IDE
    workspace is a studio of its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    A seated agent shows its terminal live on its monitor: the last lines, the
    cursor, and its name and state in the title bar. Click the monitor and the
    session opens as a window you can move, resize and type into. Send it a
    prompt, stop the current step, or use a quick order: Continue, Status?,
    Run tests or Commit. Jarvis can sharpen a prompt for you before it goes
    out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mission Control</strong> is the room for the whole fleet. Its
    launcher starts new coding agents: pick the tool, the workspace and how
    many, add a first task if you like, and drop in files for them to start
    from. You can also message several agents at once. The wall shows every
    session side by side, and the desk monitors run three parts of the app
    live: Spend, Agents and the Agentic IDE.
  </p>
  <p>
    The coding floor also lives inside the Agentic IDE, as a tab in its side
    panel. Clicking a monitor there brings that terminal forward in the grid
    right next to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Walk in, or don't</h2>
  <p>
    You get a character of your own. Move with <kbd>W</kbd>&nbsp;<kbd
      >A</kbd
    >&nbsp;<kbd>S</kbd>&nbsp;<kbd>D</kbd> or the arrow keys, hold <kbd
      >Shift</kbd
    > to run, press <kbd>Space</kbd> to jump and <kbd>E</kbd> to use whatever is
    next to you. Press <kbd>M</kbd> for the map and <kbd>H</kbd> for help and
    settings. You can also click the floor to walk there.
  </p>
  <p>
    Walking is optional. Zoom out and you get the whole floor at once, and every
    agent and every room is still one click away. Click any agent to open its
    chat, walk over, call it to you, show it on the map or add it to a new team.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to find it</h2>
  <p>
    Jarvis Verse is part of Personal Jarvis 2.4, released on 30 September 2026.
    Install or update, open <strong>Agents</strong> and switch to <strong
      >Jarvis Verse</strong
    >. In the Agentic IDE, add the <strong>Jarvis Verse</strong> tab to the side
    panel from its <strong>+</strong> menu.
  </p>
  <p>
    It runs inside the app on Windows, macOS and Linux. On a machine that
    cannot draw 3D, the agents list keeps showing every agent's state, so
    nothing is hidden behind the graphics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Jarvis Verse is brand new. If something looks wrong, or you have an idea
    for the office, <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an issue on GitHub</a> or <a
      href={DISCORD}>tell us on Discord</a
    >.
  </p>
</BlogArticle>
